\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Game Theory: Homework 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a homogenous good Bertrand duopoly (2 firms) with a market demand
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{D}=p^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}},\ p\in[0,\infty)
\]

\end_inset

Suppose that the firms can produce each unit at 0 marginal cost.
 The low price firm captures all the demand and the firms split the demand
 equally if they charge the same price.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Does this game have pure strategy Nash equilibria? If yes, what are they?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Consider now an equilibrium, in which the two firms adopt mixed strategies.
 Show that there exists a symmetric mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium of this
 game, in which each firm makes an expected profit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\pi}$
\end_inset

.
 [Hint: find the equilibrium CDF 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 and its support 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\underline{p}(\tilde{\pi}),\overline{p}(\tilde{\pi})\right]$
\end_inset

 that generates this expected payoff.]
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The profit function for firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi(p_{i},p_{j})=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if\;}\; p_{j}<p_{i}\\
\dfrac{1}{2}p_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}} & \mbox{if}\;\; p_{i}=p_{j}\\
p_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}} & \mbox{if\;}\; p_{j}>p_{i}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=p_{2}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 constitutes a pure strategy Nash equilibrium.
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=p_{2}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 is an equilibrium since neither firm can gain from deviation.
 To show its uniqueness, we need to exhaust a few possibilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}\geq p_{j}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 wants to deviate to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}=p_{j}-\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case

\series bold
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}=0,\; p_{j}>0$
\end_inset


\series default
.
 Then, firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 wants to deviate to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}=p_{j}-\varepsilon>0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=p_{2}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 is the unique pure strategy Nash equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We wish to find the equilibrium CDF and its support.
 Suppose firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is playing a mixed strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{j}$
\end_inset

 using the CDF: 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{j}(c)=P(\sigma_{j}\leq c)$
\end_inset

.
 Keeping this cdf fixed, the expected payoff for firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{i}=P(p_{i}<\sigma_{j})p_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}
\]

\end_inset

Now, for firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 to mix, it must be indifferent between all of its pure strategies.
 So, it's expected payoff must be the same for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}\in[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 Setting this expected payoff equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\pi}$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\pi}=[1-F_{j}(p_{i})]p_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}
\]

\end_inset

Solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{j}(p_{i})$
\end_inset

, we obtain an explicit expression for the CDF
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{j}(p_{i})=\begin{cases}
0 & \mbox{if}\; p_{i}\leq\tilde{\pi}^{2}\\
1-\dfrac{\tilde{\pi}}{\sqrt{p_{i}}} & \mbox{if}\; p_{i}>\tilde{\pi}^{2}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Now, let us confirm that the CDF above leads to 
\begin_inset Formula $E[\pi_{i}(p_{i},\sigma_{j})]=\tilde{\pi},\;\forall\; p_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[\pi_{i}(p_{i},\sigma_{j})\right] & = & \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}\pi_{i}(p_{i},p_{j})\ dF_{j}(p_{j})\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{p_{i}}}\pi_{i}(p_{i},p_{j})\ dF_{j}(p_{j})\ +\ {\displaystyle \intop_{p_{i}}^{\infty}}\pi_{i}(p_{i},p_{j})\ dF_{j}(p_{j})\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{p_{i}}^{\infty}}\pi_{i}(p_{i},p_{j})\ dF_{j}(p_{j})\\
 & = & \sqrt{p_{i}}\cdot{\displaystyle \intop_{p_{i}}^{\infty}}dF_{j}(p_{j})\\
 & = & \sqrt{p_{i}}\cdot\left[F_{j}(\infty)-F_{j}(p_{i})\right]\\
 & = & \sqrt{p_{i}}\cdot\left[1-\left(1-\dfrac{\tilde{\pi}}{\sqrt{p_{i}}}\right)\right]\\
 & = & \tilde{\pi}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $F_{i}(x)$
\end_inset

 listed above is the equilibrium CDF that generates expected payoff 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\pi}$
\end_inset

 with support 
\begin_inset Formula $[\tilde{\pi}^{2},\infty)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the game depicted in Figure 1, which is a three player game of
 perfect information in extensive form.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Find all the subgame-perfect Nash equilibria in pure strategies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find all Nash equilibrium outcomes that do not entail mixed strategies.
 (Note: there may be several strategy profiles which support a particular
 Nash equilibrium outcome, you only need to find one profile for each NE
 outcome.)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since player 2 and player 3 have two information sets, as well as two available
 actions at each information set, then both players can choose from a set
 containing four strategies.
 To simply notation, we will signify the set of strategies for each player
 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
S_{1} & = & \{L_{1},R_{1}\}\\
S_{2} & = & \left\{ \left(a_{2}(L_{1}),a_{2}(R_{1})\right)\right\} \\
S_{3} & = & \left\{ \left(a_{3}(L_{1},L_{2}),a_{3}(R_{1},R_{2})\right)\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, an element from player 2 or player 3's strategy set is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\left(a_{2}(L_{1}),a_{2}(R_{1})\right)\in S_{2}\\
\left(a_{3}(L_{1},L_{2}),a_{3}(R_{1},R_{2})\right)\in S_{3}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Subgame perfection assumes that players expect Nash equilibria in all subgames
 and all players expect the same equilibria.
 Thus, the set of subgame-perfect Nash equilibria is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{SPNE}\ =\ \left\{ \left(L_{1},\left[L_{2},R_{2}\right],\left[L_{3},L_{3}\right]\right),\left(R_{1},\left[R_{2},R_{2}\right],\left[R_{3},L_{3}\right]\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find all of the pure strategy Nash equilibria, we write the strategic
 form representation of this dynamic game.
\end_layout

