\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
At the third stage, players 1 and 3 are essentially moving simultaneously,
 and both decide to play a pure strategy.
 So, the pure strategy Nash Equilibria, and their corresponding payoffs,
 are listed in part (a).
 Now, at the second stage, player 2 chooses between 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
 Choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 leads to a payoff of 6, while 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 leads to one of the pure strategy Nash Equilibria of the third stage, which
 gives him a payoff of 7.
 Thus, player 2 choose 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, at the first stage, player 1 chooses between 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming the other players are rational, and all of them expect pure strategy
 Nash Equilibria to be played, player 1 can surmise their possible actions
 at later stages.
 So, choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 gives him a payoff of 7, while choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 gives him a payoff of 6.
 Therefore, using backwards induction, we have shown player 1 plays 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 in all pure strategy SPNE.
\end_layout

